// ============================================================================
// Zenith_Tools_MeshoptDecode.Tests.inl -- the golden vectors that make the
// meshopt decoder trustworthy.
//
// ★★ WHY GOLDEN VECTORS AND NOT PROPERTY CHECKS. EXT_meshopt_compression has no
// prose specification worth decoding against -- the format IS upstream
// meshoptimizer's implementation, and every one of its sharp edges (the
// vertex-FIFO index asymmetry, index codec v1's +/-1 shorthand, the 0xff
// first-corner escape) is a place where a plausible reading decodes MOST of a
// mesh correctly and then diverges. A self-consistent check -- "did it produce
// 192 indices, all in range?" -- passes on every one of them. Only a byte
// comparison against the reference implementation's own output does not.
//
// So each array below was produced by running the REFERENCE encoder and decoder
// (the `meshoptimizer` npm package, i.e. the upstream WASM build) over a fixed
// input, and the assertions are byte-for-byte. The shapes were chosen to
// exercise the parts that bite:
//
//   * 53 vertices -- NOT a multiple of the 16-byte group size, so the final
//     partial group and the block-tail seeding both run.
//   * a 64-triangle strip with periodic restarts -- exercises the edge FIFO, the
//     vertex FIFO, the codeaux table AND the free-index escapes. A pure strip or
//     a fan would leave most of the decoder unvisited.
//   * both vertex filters, encoded THROUGH the vertex codec exactly as a real
//     bufferView carries them, so the test drives the same two-step the importer
//     does rather than a filter in isolation.
//   * the same source encoded with vertex codec VERSION 1, which this decoder
//     refuses. That refusal is the contract -- gltfpack will eventually emit v1,
//     and a decoder that silently produced garbage for it would corrupt a mesh
//     with no error anywhere.
//
// ★ VERTEX CODEC v0 + INDEX CODEC v1 is not an arbitrary pairing: it is exactly
// what the gltfpack output this importer was written for carries.
//
// ★ THE INDEX GOLDEN IS THE REFERENCE DECODER'S OUTPUT, NOT THE ENCODER'S INPUT.
// The codec may rotate the corners within a triangle (kTriangleIndexOrder), so a
// round trip is not byte-equal to what went in. What the C++ decoder has to
// reproduce is what the reference decoder produces.
// ============================================================================

